# NOBORU ACCESSIBILITY RULE

Version: 1.0

Priority: HIGH

Status: AUTHORITATIVE

This rule governs accessible implementation for all Noboru UI.

Every screen, component, dialog, lesson interaction, game control, and notification surface must follow this document and the accessibility rules in uiux.mdc.

Accessible design is part of premium quality.

Not an optional polish pass.

⸻

PURPOSE

The purpose of this rule is to ensure:

* Noboru is usable by keyboard-only learners
* Screen reader users can navigate lessons, reviews, and core flows
* Motion-sensitive users are respected
* Color is never the only carrier of meaning
* Text remains readable at larger sizes
* Japanese learning UI remains accessible without sacrificing pedagogy

Learning must be reachable.

For everyone.

⸻

REQUIREMENTS

Standards Baseline

* Target WCAG 2.1 AA for core user flows
* Meet uiux.mdc requirements for screen readers, keyboard navigation, reduced motion, color blindness, high contrast, and large text

Keyboard

* All interactive controls must be reachable and operable by keyboard
* Focus order must follow visual and logical reading order
* Focus states must be visible and consistent with design tokens
* Trap focus inside modals and dialogs appropriately

Screen Readers

* Interactive elements require accessible names
* Form inputs require labels or aria-labelledby
* Dynamic content updates use appropriate live region patterns when necessary
* Decorative visuals must be hidden from assistive technology

Motion

* Honor prefers-reduced-motion
* Disable or simplify non-essential animation when reduced motion is requested
* Essential feedback may remain, but must not rely on continuous motion

Color and Contrast

* Text and interactive states must meet contrast requirements
* Do not communicate correctness, danger, or progress by color alone
* Support high contrast and dark mode token usage from app/globals.css

Touch and Mobile

* Tap targets must be large enough for mobile use
* Spacing must prevent accidental activation of adjacent controls

Language and Content

* Use lang attributes appropriately for Japanese and interface language content
* Furigana and reading aids must remain usable with assistive technology where applicable

Components

* Prefer semantic HTML before ARIA replacements
* ShadCN components must retain accessible labels, roles, and focus behavior
* Custom game and lesson controls require explicit accessibility review

Reference

* uiux.mdc ACCESSIBILITY RULES section is the UX authority for supported accommodations

⸻

CODING STANDARDS

Markup

* Use button for actions, a for navigation
* Use headings in order without skipping levels arbitrarily
* Associate errors with inputs using describedby patterns

Labels

* Visible labels preferred over aria-label alone when space allows
* Icon-only buttons require aria-label

Focus

* Do not remove focus outlines without replacement focus styling
* Restore focus after closing dialogs when appropriate

Testing

* Verify keyboard path through primary flows
* Verify screen reader name and role for custom controls
* Verify reduced motion behavior in animated components

Japanese UI

* Provide text alternatives for critical visual kanji learning aids when the visual alone would be inaccessible
* Ensure review and quiz inputs expose their purpose clearly

⸻

SUCCESS CRITERIA

Accessibility work is successful when:

* Core flows are fully keyboard operable
* Screen readers announce controls, errors, and major state changes clearly
* Reduced motion preferences are respected
* Color contrast meets baseline requirements in light and dark themes
* Icon-only controls have accessible names
* Custom lesson and game interactions remain understandable without sight-only cues
* Accessibility behavior matches uiux.mdc intent

⸻

FAILURE CRITERIA

Accessibility quality is degraded when any of the following occur:

* Keyboard traps without escape path
* Missing labels on form fields or icon buttons
* Focus lost after modal close with no restoration
* Motion-heavy feedback ignoring prefers-reduced-motion
* Success or failure indicated by color alone
* Non-semantic div click handlers without role and keyboard support
* Illegible text on mobile or in dark mode
* Furigana or reading UI that cannot be parsed by assistive technology in core flows

When failure criteria appear, fix accessibility before shipping the interaction.

⸻

EXAMPLES

Correct button

<Button aria-label="Submit answer">Submit</Button> or visible text label.

Correct modal

Dialog traps focus, closes on Escape, returns focus to trigger.

Correct reduced motion

motion components check prefers-reduced-motion and render static feedback.

Incorrect control

<div onClick={submit}>Submit</div> with no keyboard support.

Incorrect status

Green border only to indicate correct answer with no text or icon alternative.

Incorrect icon button

<Button size="icon"><X /></Button> without aria-label.

⸻

ANTI-PATTERNS

Div buttons

Removing focus outlines globally

Autoplaying motion required to understand state

Color-only error states

Inaccessible custom sliders or drag interactions without keyboard alternatives

aria-hidden on essential instructional text

Tiny tap targets on mobile drills

Animations that ignore reduced motion preferences

Inconsistent heading hierarchy across feature screens

Teaching interactions that rely solely on stroke animation with no textual alternative

END OF accessibility.mdc
